  "answer": "I cannot determine whether this particular connotation supports rejection: it has not been identified, and I cannot predict an application’s outcome. The supplied passages give the following framework instead.\n\n**Act — statutory test:** The question is not simply whether the connotation is ‘strong’. Section 43 requires likely deception or confusion caused by the connotation when the trade mark is used for the particular goods or services. [TMA1995/s43]\n\n**Manual — examination practice, not legislation:**\n• A mere possibility of deception or confusion at an indeterminate time is insufficient; assess the application in the current marketplace. [TMM/Part29/3/3/2]\n• Consider the connotation arising from the whole trade mark or a sign within it. [TMM/Part20/5/5/5]\n• Compare the connotation with the specification. The Manual says a descriptive term does not trigger section 43 where the described goods/services are covered, but a connotation directly contradicted by the specification presents a high likelihood of deception or confusion. [TMM/Part29/4#1]\n• Consider whether even a clear connotation would be understood as fanciful or unrealistic. [TMM/Part29/4#1]\n\nAccordingly, the relevant enquiry is whether the connotation creates the statutory likelihood in relation to the claimed goods or services—not its strength in isolation. [TMA1995/s43]"
